<article class="env-lemma" id="01OW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01OW">Lemma <span data-tag="01OW">28.5.2</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The scheme $X$ is locally Noetherian. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every affine open $U \subset X$ the ring $\mathcal{O}_ X(U)$ is Noetherian. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
There exists an affine open covering $X = \bigcup U_ i$ such that each $\mathcal{O}_ X(U_ i)$ is Noetherian. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
There exists an open covering $X = \bigcup X_ j$ such that each open subscheme $X_ j$ is locally Noetherian. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Moreover, if $X$ is locally Noetherian then every open subscheme is locally Noetherian. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      To show this it suffices to show that being Noetherian is a local property of rings, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01OR" data-tag="01OR">28.4.3</a>. Any localization of a Noetherian ring is Noetherian, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00FN" data-tag="00FN">10.31.1</a>. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00EO" data-tag="00EO">10.23.2</a> we see the second property to Definition <a href="/tag/01OP" data-tag="01OP">28.4.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>